D3 Movie Genre Trends Over Time Description of Visualization: This visualization performs a medium-level task - comparing movie genre trends over time (1990-2017). The marks are lines showing each genre's trajectory, filled areas beneath the lines, and circles that appear on hover. The channels are x-position for year, y-position for movie count, and color for genre type (e.g., green for Drama, blue for Comedy). The design uses layered elements: semi-transparent areas in the background and solid lines in the foreground, with a vertical tracking line and tooltip appearing on mouse hover to show exact values. Users can click legend items to show/hide genres, zoom to specific time periods using buttons, and watch an initial animation that draws the lines from left to right. The visualization follows standard practices like using consistent colors throughout, adding grid lines for reference, and applying smooth curves to make trends easier to follow. This multi-line chart design allows simultaneous comparison of all ten genres' popularity patterns over the 27-year period.

Genre Success by Release Month Heatmap: This heatmap visualization performs a medium level task by identifying seasonal patterns in movie genre revenue performance. The marks are rectangular cells in a grid layout, with text annotations showing exact values. The channels are x-position for release month (categorical), y-position for genre (categorical), and color saturation/hue for average revenue (quantitative) using a custom diverging color scheme from blue through light green/yellow to orange/red. The design displays average revenue in millions for genre-month combinations with at least 5 movies to ensure statistical reliability. The visualization uses a sequential color map that transitions from cool to warm colors as revenue increases, making high-performing combinations (like Animation in May at $498M) immediately visible through intense red coloring. Gray gridlines separate cells for clarity, and bold annotations ensure values are readable directly without referencing the color bar. The heatmap structure enables two key comparisons: horizontal scanning shows which months work best for each genre (e.g., summer months for Adventure/Animation), while vertical scanning reveals which genres dominate specific months. This design effectively reveals seasonal release strategies, showing that blockbuster genres like Animation and Adventure perform best in early summer, while smaller genres maintain more consistent performance year-round.
